00:01:34,474 --> 00:02:04,994
This is step 5, page 5. This is the most important eureka moment in this proof! Look at the new green line segment drawn on the shape. It is an auxiliary line that divides a large square with one side (a+b) into four congruent right triangles and a square in the middle (area c squared). A relationship that was not visible through the original shape alone is divided into two comparable shapes by this single green auxiliary line!

7
00:02:04,994 --> 00:02:32,682
This is step 6, page 6. Now, combine the geometric clues we checked earlier into one. From the correspondence between shapes, we can confirm that the Pythagorean theorem and the area conservation conclusion that the sum of the squares of the two sides with right angles that we wanted to prove are always equal to the square of the hypotenuse (a squared plus b squared = c squared) are inevitably true.

