00:02:32,682 --> 00:03:04,762
This is step 7, page 7. Look at the final mark highlighted in red on the shape! Since the size of the corresponding parts becomes the same due to the congruence and symmetry properties of the shapes proven in the previous step, the Pythagorean theorem and area conservation conclusion that the sum of the squares of the two sides with right angles, which we were aiming for, is always equal to the square of the hypotenuse (square of a plus square of b = square of c) have finally been proven!

9
00:03:04,762 --> 00:04:24,295
The final step 8, the proof, is complete! Starting from the geometric starting conditions in which a square is drawn with the three sides a, b, c (hypotenuse c) of a right triangle as one side and each side as the starting condition, the sum of the squares of the two sides containing the right angle is always equal to the square of the hypotenuse (the square of a (plus b squared = c squared) clearly confirmed that the Pythagorean theorem and the area conservation conclusion hold. The key point to remember here is that the center square can be a rhombus just because the four sides have the same length c, so it is perfect to prove that the interior angle is 90 degrees (a square) by using the sum of the two acute angles to be 90 degrees! Finally, ask a deeper question. If we place an equilateral triangle or a semicircle instead of a square on the three sides of a right triangle, will the sum of the areas of the two smaller shapes still be the same as the area of ​​the hypotenuse shape? Experiment by changing the values ​​and shapes directly on the math homepage in the link in the description!
